    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band">
        <h1>fetch_forge <span class="t-red">quick start</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Cached, retryable, reactive remote data on top of signals. This guide
          covers reads with Query and QueryClient, one-shot writes with
          Mutation, and the Flutter widgets, then two day-one recipes and an
          honest list of what the package leaves out.
        </p>
        <div class="meta">
          <span class="ver">0.3.0</span>
          <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/fetch_forge">pub.dev</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/albetnov/forge-flutter/tree/main/packages/fetch_forge">README source</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ol class="toc" aria-label="Stops on this line">
        <li><a href="#install"><span class="stop">S1</span>Install the line</a></li>
        <li><a href="#query"><span class="stop">S2</span>Reads: Query and QueryClient</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mutation"><span class="stop">S3</span>Writes: Mutation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#flutter"><span class="stop">S4</span>In Flutter widgets</a></li>
        <li><a href="#recipes"><span class="stop">S5</span>Two day-one recipes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#scope"><span class="stop">S6</span>What is not included</a></li>
        <li><a href="#skills"><span class="stop">S7</span>Agent skills</a></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="prose">
        <section class="block" id="install" aria-labelledby="install-h">
          <h2 id="install-h">Install the line</h2>
          <p>
            From pub.dev, with <code class="inline">signals</code> alongside:
            a query's state <em>is</em> signal state, so the dependency is
            structural, not incidental:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>pubspec.yaml</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-install">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-install" set:html={highlight(installYaml)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Working from this monorepo instead, point at the path:</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>pubspec.yaml, monorepo checkout</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-install-path">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-install-path" set:html={highlight(installPath)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Run the full walkthrough from the package itself:</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>cli walkthrough</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-cli">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-cli" set:html={highlight(cliCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Note:</span> <code class="inline">--enable-asserts</code> turns on the cache-key guard, a debug-only check by design. Flutter debug builds already have assertions on.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="query" aria-labelledby="query-h">
          <h2 id="query-h">Reads: Query and QueryClient</h2>
          <p>
            A <code class="inline">Query</code> turns a <code class="inline">V → Future&lt;T&gt;</code> fetcher into a value that lives in the reactive graph. Everything it exposes (status, data, last error) reads through <code class="inline">.value</code>; everything it does (refetch, invalidate, seed) is a method that writes to those signals.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>hello world</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-hello">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-hello" set:html={highlight(queryHello)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Bind once, derive freely</h3>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">bind(variables)</code> is the effect you would otherwise write by hand, with one difference: the query owns it. <code class="inline">dispose()</code> releases the binding and the signals together, and every field stays a <code class="inline">ReadonlySignal</code>. As a result, <code class="inline">computed</code> and <code class="inline">effect</code> compose with a query the way they compose with <code class="inline">int</code>.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>signals are the API</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-signals">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-signals" set:html={highlight(querySignals)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Variables are the cache key</h3>
          <p>
            Two variables are the same request when they are <code class="inline">==</code>. Records work out of the box; a class must override <code class="inline">==</code> and <code class="inline">hashCode</code>.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>value-type variables</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-vars">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-vars" set:html={highlight(queryVars)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Footgun:</span> a <code class="inline">List</code>, <code class="inline">Map</code>, or <code class="inline">Set</code> compares by identity, so equal contents never match in the cache. The query refuses them in debug builds with a <code class="inline">CacheKeyError</code> that names the fix: pass a record, a value type, or map the variables with <code class="inline">cacheKey</code>.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>mapping the key</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-cachekey">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-cachekey" set:html={highlight(queryCacheKey)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Share a client</h3>
          <p>
            A <code class="inline">QueryClient</code> is an optional shared cache keyed by query variables. Give queries the same client and the same key mapper and they find each other's entries. Concurrent fetches for one page deduplicate instead of doubling.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>one fetch per key</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-client">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-client" set:html={highlight(queryClient)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="mutation" aria-labelledby="mutation-h">
          <h2 id="mutation-h">Writes: Mutation</h2>
          <p>
            A mutation is a one-shot write with the same signal shape as a query (status, data, error), but it never fetches on its own, is never deduplicated, and speaks <code class="inline">pending</code> instead of <code class="inline">loading</code>. There is intentionally no snapshot: a write has no staleness, so branching on pending, success, and error is the whole UI.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>fire, or await</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-mut">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-mut" set:html={highlight(mutBasic)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">mutate</code> never throws: failure lands on <code class="inline">error</code>. <code class="inline">mutateAsync</code> rethrows, like calling the API directly. Pass a <code class="inline">cacheDuration</code> and successful results are written to the client under the variables' key, where a query reading the same key finds them without fetching. That closes the write-then-refetch loop with the mutation knowing nothing about queries:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>write, then refetch</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-mutloop">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-mutloop" set:html={highlight(mutLoop)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="flutter" aria-labelledby="flutter-h">
          <h2 id="flutter-h">In Flutter widgets</h2>
          <p>
            The Flutter entrypoint (<code class="inline">package:fetch_forge/flutter.dart</code>) re-exports everything above and adds three widgets. <code class="inline">QueryBuilder</code> owns a query, binds it to a variables signal, and rebuilds from the <code class="inline">QueryView</code> it hands to its builder; <code class="inline">QueryScope</code> shares one client with a subtree, so two builders watching the same variables share one fetch and settle together; <code class="inline">MutationBuilder</code> owns a mutation and rebuilds from its <code class="inline">MutationView</code>.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>builder and scope</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fq">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fq" set:html={highlight(fqBuilder)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            A <code class="inline">refresh</code> signal forces a refetch of the current variables when bumped. When the widget's arguments change, the old query no longer describes the request, so the state disposes it and builds a new one; unmount disposes bindings and query together. Keep <code class="inline">fetcher</code> referentially stable (a top-level function or tear-off, not an inline closure): a new fetcher identity recreates the query with no data, so a parent rebuilding per keystroke flashes the subtree back to loading. Only <code class="inline">builder</code> may be inline. The layer stays thin: nothing here you could not write with <code class="inline">Query</code>, <code class="inline">bind</code>, and <code class="inline">SignalBuilder</code> in about twenty lines, and the core never imports Flutter.
          </p>
          <h3>Fetching on demand</h3>
          <p>
            The view is a handle, not just a snapshot: it carries the state of the read and the calls that drive it — <code class="inline">watch</code>, <code class="inline">refetch</code>, <code class="inline">setData</code>, <code class="inline">invalidate</code> — so a button can ask for fresh data without a widget owning the query by hand. Set <code class="inline">autoFetch</code> to <code class="inline">false</code> and the variables signal is not bound at all: nothing fetches on mount, and nothing fetches on a variables change either. The read stays idle until an explicit call asks for it.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>tap to fetch</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fqa">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fqa" set:html={highlight(fqManual)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Writing from a widget</h3>
          <p>
            Writes have the same shape. <code class="inline">MutationBuilder</code> owns the mutation, hands its builder a <code class="inline">MutationView</code> — the mutation's signals plus the calls that start a write — and disposes everything on unmount, so no widget owns a mutation by hand.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>write from a widget</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fqm">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fqm" set:html={highlight(fqMutation)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            A <code class="inline">trigger</code> signal starts the write declaratively: any bump runs it with the current <code class="inline">variables</code>, so a parent drives the write by bumping a signal instead of wiring a callback. The first run is skipped — a trigger is a bump, not an initial value — and the variables are read without subscribing, so a variables change alone never writes.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>a trigger drives the write</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fqt">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fqt" set:html={highlight(fqTrigger)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>run the demo app</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-demo">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-demo" set:html={highlight(demoCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="recipes" aria-labelledby="recipes-h">
          <h2 id="recipes-h">Two day-one recipes</h2>
          <p>
            The README collects four things people ask for on day one, none needing library support. Two sampled here; the polling and resume timers follow the same shape: a signal you own plus <code class="inline">refetch</code>.
          </p>
          <h3>Optimistic update</h3>
          <p>Seed the cache, then let the write fall where it may. If it fails, roll back by seeding again with the previous value.</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>seed first, reconcile after</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-opt">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-opt" set:html={highlight(recipeOpt)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Invalidate and refetch after a write</h3>
          <p>The mutation remembers the keys it wrote, so invalidating them is one call, and every query watching one refetches through the shared client.</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>one call, every watcher refetches</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-inv">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-inv" set:html={highlight(recipeInv)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="scope" aria-labelledby="scope-h">
          <h2 id="scope-h">What is not included</h2>
          <p>
            Each omission below is a deliberate line: the thing it replaces is a few lines of your code, and a library version would be hidden policy.
          </p>
          <table class="scope">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Background polling</th><td>A timer you own plus <code class="inline">refetch</code> is three lines; a library timer is policy.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Refetch on resume, focus, reconnect</th><td>All three are a signal bump plus one effect, which is why the core has no lifecycle listener to get wrong.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Tag-based invalidation</th><td>A version signal plus <code class="inline">invalidate</code> covers it, without a tag registry in the core.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Persistence, offline queues, cancellation</th><td>A storage engine, a retry queue, or an abort signal bolted on; none belongs behind value equality.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Suspense bridges</th><td><code class="inline">AsyncValue</code> is a plain value, so a bridge to Riverpod or Bloc is a ten-line adapter you should own.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Automatic cache GC</th><td>Timers are policy. Expiry is checked on read; <code class="inline">clear</code> reclaims memory on demand.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">GraphQL, codegen, schemas</th><td>Out of scope by design; the fetcher is any <code class="inline">V → Future&lt;T&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Rule of thumb, from the README:</span> if something cannot be built from the public API in under twenty lines, file an issue: that is a gap in the API, not a feature to add quietly.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
          <h2 id="skills-h">Agent skills</h2>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">fetch_forge</code> also ships its guidance as
            agent skills: prescriptive rules and examples authored for Cursor,
            Claude Code, Copilot, Cline, and any other agent working in your
            repository. Each skill is a <code class="inline">SKILL.md</code>
            shipped inside the package, so the advice is versioned with the API
            you actually installed.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
